INT. INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

A bare room. A steel table bolted to the floor. A one-way mirror. A single chair on each side.

AGENT MAREN SOLOMON, 40s, sits with a manila folder squared in front of her and does not open it. Her sleeves are pressed. Her face gives nothing.

Opposite her, wrists in plastic ties, a man in a gray hoodie. THEO VANCE, 30s. He studies the table, then the mirror, then her hands.

MAREN
Do you want water?

THEO
No.

MAREN
Coffee?

THEO
No.

MAREN
You've been here six hours.

THEO
Eight.

She glances at her watch. He is right. She makes no remark.

MAREN (CONT'D)
Eight.

THEO
You don't believe me.

MAREN
I believe your watch is better than mine.

He tilts his head, almost smiling. He lifts his bound wrists to scratch his nose. She watches the movement closely, then looks away.

MAREN (CONT'D)
Tell me about the drive.

THEO
What drive?

MAREN
The one in your sock.

His smile goes. He lowers his hands. Under the table, a shoe taps twice and stops.

THEO
I don't have a drive.

MAREN
We have a photograph of the drive.

THEO
Then you don't need me.

She opens the folder at last. Slides a single glossy photo across the steel. He doesn't look at it. His eyes stay on her.

MAREN
Who gave it to you?

THEO
Nobody.

MAREN
(simultaneously)
Who gave it to you?

^THEO
(simultaneously)
Nobody gave it.

Silence. The ventilation fan clicks on, then off.

Maren reaches across and, slowly, takes his hand in both of hers, as if comforting him. Turns it over. Examines the palm. A faint blue smear of ink on the heel of it.

She lets go. He pulls the hand back, and tucks it under his other arm.

MAREN (CONT'D)
That's from the stamp at the locker.

Theo says nothing. The shoe starts tapping again.

Maren closes the folder. She stands, tucks her chair beneath the table, and goes to the door. At the threshold she turns.

MAREN (CONT'D)
Eight hours, Mr. Vance. I think you'd like a coffee now.

THEO
(low)
Black.
